---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { categories, formatNumber, href, markers, meta, products } from '../lib/data.ts';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';

const f = meta.funnel;
const rejected = (key: string) => formatNumber(f.rejected[key] ?? 0);
const exportDate = meta.exportDate
  ? new Date(meta.exportDate).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' })
  : null;
---

<Base
  title="About the data and how this site works"
  description={`Where the NOVA groups on ${SITE.name} come from, how the ${formatNumber(products.length)} products were chosen, and what the data can and cannot tell you.`}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About the data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {SITE.name} is a static reference site. It does not make its own judgements about food: every NOVA group,
      ingredient list and category comes from Open Food Facts, and this site explains and compares them.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org/">Open Food Facts</a> is a non-profit, collaborative database of food
      products from around the world, built by volunteers who scan barcodes and photograph labels. It is published under
      the <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/">Open Database License (ODbL)</a>, which allows reuse
      with attribution. Product photos belong to their contributors and are licensed under
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each product page links to the product's entry on Open Food Facts. If something is wrong or out of date, correcting
      it there fixes it for everyone, including this site at its next update.
    </p>

    <h2>How the products were chosen</h2>
    <p>
      The site is rebuilt from the full Open Food Facts export{exportDate ? `; this version uses the export of ${exportDate}` : ''}.
      A product is included only if it has everything needed for a useful page:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption class="visually-hidden">How many products passed each step</caption>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Products in the export</th><td class="num">{formatNumber(f.linesRead)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">No NOVA group computed</th><td class="num">−{rejected('no-nova-group')}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Label not in English</th><td class="num">−{rejected('not-english')}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">No category</th><td class="num">−{rejected('no-category')}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">No usable product name</th><td class="num">−{rejected('no-name')}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">No parsed ingredient list</th><td class="num">−{rejected('no-ingredients')}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Other problems (withdrawn products, invalid barcodes, unreadable records)</th><td class="num">−{formatNumber((f.rejected['obsolete'] ?? 0) + (f.rejected['bad-code'] ?? 0) + f.parseErrors)}</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><th scope="row">Eligible products</th><td class="num">{formatNumber(f.eligible)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Most-scanned eligible products considered</th><td class="num">{formatNumber(f.pooled)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">After merging duplicates (same brand and name)</th><td class="num">{formatNumber(f.afterDedupe)}</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><th scope="row">Products on this site</th><td class="num">{formatNumber(f.selected)}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Products are ranked by how many people have scanned them with the Open Food Facts app recently, so the site covers
      the foods people actually look up. Of the {formatNumber(f.selected)} products, {formatNumber(f.selectedForBalance)} were
      added specifically because they are less processed than a popular product in the same category. Without them,
      many popular products would have no alternative to point to.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site also has {formatNumber(categories.length)} category pages, for categories with enough products to
      compare, and {formatNumber(markers.length)} pages on individual ingredients and additives.
    </p>

    <h2>What the NOVA group means here</h2>
    <p>
      NOVA groups are computed automatically by Open Food Facts from crowdsourced data, so they are estimates. A missing
      ingredient, a typo or an outdated recipe can put a product in the wrong group. Read the
      <a href={href('what-is-nova/')}>explanation of NOVA</a> for how the groups are defined and what they do and do not
      tell you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rankings within a category ("least processed first") are our own: products are sorted by NOVA group, then by the
      number of ultra-processing markers, then by the number of additives and ingredients. That order is a convenience
      for comparison, not a health score.
    </p>

    <h2>Health disclaimer</h2>
    <p>
      This site provides general information about food processing. It is not medical, nutritional or dietary advice and
      should not replace advice from a doctor, dietitian or other qualified professional. Always read the label on the
      product itself, especially if you have an allergy or intolerance.
    </p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      This site may show advertising to cover its costs. Advertisers have no influence over which products appear or how
      they are described.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  table {
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
  tr.total th,
  tr.total td {
    font-weight: 700;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }
</style>
